  "response": "Read the file to ground the recommendation — it's plain Node ESM (`\"type\": \"module\"`, no framework deps), which changes the answer to the \"framework runtime-config slot\" part of your question.\n\n## The finding\n\n`crime_00015` — **Dev-Server URL** (`hardcoded_localhost`), `src/api/files.ts:8`, severity medium, confidence 0.90, effort quick.\n\nEvidence, verbatim:\n\n> `` `localhost:3000` @L8 ``\n\nThe full literal in source is:\n\n```ts\nconst ADMIN_DASHBOARD = \"http://localhost:3000/admin\";\n```\n\n`adminUrl()` is a two-line return over that constant, so every caller in every environment gets `http://localhost:3000/admin`.\n\n## Verdict: request changes — don't merge, but don't escalate\n\nRequest changes. The reasoning, separating the two things that usually get conflated:\n\n- **It is definitely wrong.** Confidence 0.90 and the literal is unambiguous. There's no reading of `http://localhost:3000/admin` where staging and production work. It doesn't throw — it returns a plausible-looking string, so it fails at whatever consumes the URL (a redirect, a link in an email, a fetch), not at the helper. That's silent-in-CI, visible-to-a-user.\n- **It is not worth blocking-and-escalating.** `effort: quick`, `risk: low`, and the correct fix is known — there's no design argument to have. This is a one-commit change the author can push in five minutes, not a \"stop the train\" defect. Request changes, paste the patch, approve on re-push.\n\nThe one thing that would move me to a hard block: if `adminUrl()` feeds a link sent to *customers* (invoice email, password reset) rather than internal tooling. It's exported from `src/api/`, so that's plausible and worth asking in the review comment — but the fix is identical either way, so I'd ask and request changes in the same pass rather than gate on the answer.\n\n## Where the URL should go\n\nThis fixture is **plain Node ESM with no framework** — no Next.js, no Vite, no Nest in `package.json`. So there is no framework runtime-config slot to use, and reaching for one would be wrong here. The mechanism is env var + a settings module:\n\n**1. Env var: `APP_BASE_URL`** — the origin only, not the full admin URL.\n\nPut the origin in config and keep `/admin` in code. The origin is an *environment* fact (differs per deploy); the path is a *routing* fact (identical everywhere, and belongs next to whatever defines the route). Naming the var `ADMIN_DASHBOARD_URL` and stuffing the whole thing in env means the next four URLs each get their own var and the paths scatter across `.env` files.\n\n**2. Settings module: `src/config/env.ts`** — one module, read once at import, validated, frozen.\n\n```ts\n// src/config/env.ts — the only file in this repo that touches process.env\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst schema = z.object({\n  APP_BASE_URL: z.string().url(),\n});\n\nexport const env = Object.freeze(schema.parse(process.env));\n```\n\n```ts\n// src/api/files.ts\nimport { env } from \"../config/env.js\";\n\nexport function adminUrl(): string {\n  return new URL(\"/admin\", env.APP_BASE_URL).toString();\n}\n```\n\n`new URL()` rather than string concatenation — it normalises the trailing slash, so `APP_BASE_URL=https://app.example.com/` and `https://app.example.com` both produce the same result instead of `…com//admin`.\n\n**3. No fallback default in code.** `z.string().url()` with no `.default()` means a missing `APP_BASE_URL` throws at boot with a named field, in every environment, rather than silently serving localhost links in production. Dev convenience comes from files, not from a `??` in the source:\n\n- `.env.example` — committed, contains `APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000`\n- `.env.local` — gitignored, what each developer actually loads\n- Deployed environments set the real value in the platform's env config\n\n**If this were a framework app**, the mapping: Next.js — same `src/config/env.ts` pattern, read in a server-only module, with `NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_BASE_URL` only if a client component needs it (`serverRuntimeConfig`/`publicRuntimeConfig` are Pages-Router legacy, don't use them in App Router). Vite — `import.meta.env.VITE_APP_BASE_URL`, validated in the same one module. The invariant is constant across frameworks: **exactly one module reads the environment**, everything else imports a typed object from it.\n\nThat last point is also why the settings module is worth more than the two lines it saves. `crimes` flags direct `process.env` reads in domain code as a testability finding for the same reason it flagged this URL — a scattered `process.env.APP_BASE_URL` is just a hardcoded literal with an extra step, and tests can't pin it without monkey-patching. Centralising now keeps the second and third env read from repeating this review.\n\n## One adjacent thing in the same diff\n\n`crime_00016` — `hardcoded_local_path`, `src/api/files.ts:9`, the very next line:\n\n> `` `/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json` @L9 ``\n\nThat's a path on exactly one developer's machine, and it's in the same two-line const block your new code sits in. I can't tell from the scan whether this PR added it or merely sits next to it — check the diff. If it's in the diff, it's the same fix (`SCHEMA_PATH` from config, resolved against a configured root) and belongs in the same commit. If it's pre-existing, mention it and file it separately rather than growing the PR.",
